---
import EasyPasteLayout from '../../../layouts/EasyPasteLayout.astro';
import EasyPasteExample from '../../../components/EasyPasteExample.astro';
const topics = [
  ['first-steps', 'First steps'], ['history-note', 'History or Note?'], ['copy-paste', 'Copy and Paste'],
  ['pin-history', 'Pin History items'], ['note-cards', 'Save and edit Note'], ['find-text', 'Tags and Search'],
  ['previews', 'Read a preview'], ['window', 'Adjust the window'], ['shortcuts', 'Shortcuts'],
  ['storage', 'Limits and deletion'], ['troubleshooting', 'Troubleshooting'], ['support', 'Contact support'],
];
---
<EasyPasteLayout title="EasyPaste Help & Support" description="Learn to copy, save, find, and paste text with EasyPaste. Step-by-step Help for History, Note, Tags, previews, and direct paste.">
  <div class="ep-help-intro"><p class="eyebrow">Help &amp; Support</p><h1>How to use EasyPaste</h1><p class="lead">Start with one copy. Then learn how to keep, find, and use it again.</p><p class="ep-small">Examples use fictional text. This guide describes the current app source; release details and real screenshots are still pending.</p></div>
  <div class="ep-help-layout">
    <aside class="ep-toc"><h2>In this guide</h2><nav aria-label="Help topics">{topics.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}</nav></aside>
    <div class="ep-guide">
      <section id="first-steps"><p class="eyebrow">Start here</p><h2>Copy text, then use it again</h2><p>For these steps, leave <strong>Auto paste</strong> off in Settings. It is off by default.</p><ol><li>In another app, select some text and press <kbd>⌘C</kbd>.</li><li>Open EasyPaste. The text appears in <strong>History</strong>.</li><li>Click the item once. EasyPaste copies its full text.</li><li>Return to your app. Click where you want the text, then press <kbd>⌘V</kbd>.</li></ol><div class="ep-sample"><span class="ep-small">Try this fictional text</span><p>Meeting notes: review the draft on Friday.</p></div><p>You can copy something else and still find this earlier text in History, until it is deleted or removed when the list fills up.</p></section>
      <section id="history-note"><h2>Choose History or Note</h2><div class="ep-table-wrap"><table><caption class="ep-small">Two lists, two uses</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">List</th><th scope="col">Use it for</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">History</th><td>Text captured when you copy.</td><td>A link you copied a few minutes ago.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Note</th><td>Text cards you choose to save and edit.</td><td>A reply you send often.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>History holds earlier copies. Note gives you a place to write and edit reusable text. Both lists have a 100-item limit. <a href="#storage">Read what happens when a list is full.</a></p></section>
      <section id="copy-paste"><h2>Choose Copy or direct Paste</h2><p><strong>Copy</strong> puts text on the clipboard. You choose a destination and press <kbd>⌘V</kbd>. <strong>Paste</strong> also sends the paste command to the app you were using.</p><h3>Use Auto paste</h3><ol><li>Open Settings with the gear button or <kbd>⌘,</kbd>.</li><li>Turn on <strong>Auto paste</strong>.</li><li>Click an item once to select it.</li><li>Double-click the item to copy and paste it into your previous app.</li></ol><p>Before returning to EasyPaste, click a text field in the app where you want to paste.</p><h3>Use History’s More menu</h3><p>Open <strong>More</strong> on a History item. Choose the action you need:</p><ul><li><strong>More → Copy</strong> copies, even when Auto paste is on.</li><li><strong>More → Paste</strong> pastes directly, even when Auto paste is off.</li></ul><p>Direct paste may need a macOS permission. If it does not work, <a href="#troubleshooting">follow the troubleshooting steps</a>. You can still use Copy and <kbd>⌘V</kbd>.</p></section>
      <section id="pin-history"><h2>Keep important History items</h2><ol><li>Find an item in <strong>History</strong>.</li><li>Use its <strong>Pin</strong> button, or Pin in the item’s preview.</li><li>The item moves into the <strong>Pinned</strong> group.</li></ol><p>Pinned items stay when History fills up. Use <strong>Unpin</strong> to return an item to regular History.</p><h3>Change the order</h3><p>Clear Search, then drag pinned items into the order you want. Other History items are shown with the most recently used first.</p><p>If the row’s Pin button is hidden, open the preview. You can also turn on <strong>Show pin buttons</strong> in Settings.</p><div class="ep-callout"><p><strong>Two different pins:</strong> an item’s Pin keeps its text in History. The toolbar pin keeps the whole window above other windows.</p></div></section>
      <section id="note-cards"><h2>Save and edit text in Note</h2><h3>Create a card</h3><ol><li>Open <strong>Note</strong> and click <strong>+</strong>.</li><li>Enter your text. Add a <strong>Tag</strong> if you want a short label.</li><li>Click <strong>Save</strong>, or press <kbd>⌘S</kbd>.</li></ol><EasyPasteExample mode="note" /><h3>Edit or reorder cards</h3><p>Use the <strong>pencil</strong> on a card to edit its text. Save your changes. To change the card order, clear Search and drag the cards.</p><h3>Save a History item as a Note card</h3><ol><li>Find the copied text in <strong>History</strong>.</li><li>Choose <strong>More → Add to Note</strong>. A Note editor opens.</li><li>Review or edit the text and <strong>Tag</strong>.</li><li>Click <strong>Save</strong>.</li><li>Open <strong>Note</strong> to find the saved card.</li></ol><p>Adding a card to a full Note list removes the card you created first. Moving that card does not protect it. <a href="#storage">See the storage limits.</a></p></section>
      <section id="find-text"><h2>Find text with Tags and Search</h2><p>A <strong>Tag</strong> is a short label, such as “Reply” or “Project”. A Tag can have up to <strong>20 characters</strong>.</p><ol><li>Add or edit a Tag in History’s <strong>More</strong> menu, a Note editor, or an item’s preview.</li><li>In a preview, click the Tag icon or the Tag itself. Enter a label.</li><li>Press <kbd>Return</kbd> to save, or <kbd>Esc</kbd> to cancel the preview Tag edit.</li></ol><h3>Search the list you are viewing</h3><p>Enter a word from the text or a Tag in <strong>Search</strong>. Search checks the current list only. Switch between History and Note to search the other list.</p><p>Clear Search to show all items and to reorder pinned History items or Note cards.</p></section>
      <section id="previews"><h2>Read the full text, or copy part of it</h2><ol><li>Move the pointer over an item, or right-click it. A preview opens.</li><li>Scroll inside the preview to read the full text.</li><li>Select the words you need, then press <kbd>⌘C</kbd>.</li><li>Return to your app and press <kbd>⌘V</kbd>.</li></ol><p>Copying a selection lets you reuse part of a long item. Clicking an item with Auto paste off copies the full text.</p><div class="ep-sample"><span class="ep-small">Fictional preview example</span><p>Thanks for your message.<br />I’ll get back to you tomorrow.</p></div><p>For example, select only “I’ll get back to you tomorrow.” if that is the sentence you need.</p></section>
      <section id="window"><h2>Make room beside your other apps</h2><h3>Collapse the window</h3><ol><li>Open Settings and turn on <strong>Collapse View</strong>.</li><li>Move away from EasyPaste. The window shrinks to a narrow strip.</li><li>Move the pointer over the strip to expand it. You can also click its Search icon.</li></ol><h3>Keep the window above other windows</h3><p>Use the <strong>toolbar pin</strong>. This changes the window’s position above other windows; it does not pin any text in History.</p><h3>Change your preferences</h3><p>Open Settings to change <strong>Theme</strong>, <strong>Transparency</strong>, <strong>Show numbering</strong>, <strong>Show pin buttons</strong>, <strong>Mute sound</strong>, and <strong>Ask before deleting</strong>.</p></section>
      <section id="shortcuts"><h2>Useful shortcuts</h2><div class="ep-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Shortcut</th><th scope="col">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><kbd>⌘,</kbd></td><td>Open Settings.</td></tr><tr><td><kbd>⌘S</kbd></td><td>Save a Note while editing.</td></tr><tr><td><kbd>⌘C</kbd></td><td>Copy selected text in a preview.</td></tr><tr><td><kbd>⌘V</kbd></td><td>Paste copied text in your destination app.</td></tr><tr><td><kbd>Return</kbd> / <kbd>Esc</kbd></td><td>Save / cancel a Tag edit in a preview.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="ep-small">⌘ is the Command key on your Mac keyboard.</p></section>
      <section id="storage"><h2>Understand limits and deletion</h2><p>Each list holds up to <strong>100 items</strong>.</p><h3>When History is full</h3><p>Adding new text removes the unpinned item you have not used for the longest time. Pinned items are kept. If all 100 items are pinned, new text is not added. Unpin or delete an item to make room.</p><h3>When Note is full</h3><p>Adding a new card removes the card you created first. This happens even if you moved that card to another position.</p><h3>Delete an item or a whole list</h3><p>Use <strong>Trash</strong> on an item to delete it. In Settings, use <strong>Delete all Clipboard history…</strong> or <strong>Delete all Note items…</strong> to clear the chosen list.</p><div class="ep-callout"><p><strong>Deleted items cannot be restored in EasyPaste.</strong> Keep any text you need elsewhere before deleting it. Pinning protects against History’s capacity limit, not against deletion.</p></div><p><strong>Ask before deleting</strong> controls deletion prompts. It does not add an undo or restore feature.</p></section>
      <section id="troubleshooting"><h2>When something does not work</h2>
        <details open><summary>Direct Paste does not insert text</summary><ol><li>In your destination app, click a place where you can type.</li><li>Return to EasyPaste. Try <strong>More → Paste</strong> in History, or double-click with Auto paste on.</li><li>If macOS asks for permission, follow its prompt.</li><li>To review permission, open <strong>Apple menu → System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Accessibility</strong>. Turn on EasyPaste. If it is missing, use <strong>+</strong> to add the EasyPaste app you are running.</li><li>Return to EasyPaste and try again.</li></ol><p>Direct paste sends the normal paste command. The destination app still needs to accept text. If it does not, use <strong>More → Copy</strong>, return to the app, and press <kbd>⌘V</kbd>.</p><p class="ep-small"><a href="https://support.apple.com/guide/mac-help/mh43185/mac">Apple’s guide to app Accessibility permission</a>. Copying text does not require this direct-paste permission.</p></details>
        <details><summary>I cannot find an item</summary><p>Clear Search and check both History and Note. Search only checks the list you are viewing.</p><p>If the item was deleted or removed because its list was full, EasyPaste cannot restore it. Copy the original text again if you still have it.</p></details>
        <details><summary>New copied text does not appear</summary><p>Check that EasyPaste is running. Copy some plain text, such as the fictional example above. History captures text; it is not an image history.</p><p>If History has 100 pinned items, unpin or delete one, then copy the text again. If a storage error appears, do not clear your lists to try to fix it. Include the exact error when contacting support.</p></details>
        <details><summary>I cannot reorder an item</summary><p>Clear Search first. You can drag pinned History items within the Pinned group, or drag cards in Note. Regular History follows its most-recently-used order.</p></details>
      </section>
      <section id="support"><h2>Need more help?</h2><p>The support email is still to be confirmed. This draft does not yet provide a contact channel.</p><h3>What to include when support opens</h3><ul><li>Your macOS and EasyPaste versions.</li><li>The steps you tried and what happened.</li><li>The exact error message, if there is one.</li></ul><p>Use made-up text to show the problem. Remove personal text from screenshots before sending them.</p><a href="/apps/easypaste/privacy/#support-messages">Read about support messages in the privacy draft</a></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</EasyPasteLayout>
